00:05:40.627 --> 00:05:41.960
MISHA BRUKMAN: Thank you, Steve.

00:05:45.685 --> 00:05:48.060
So let's talk about Google
Cloud Bigtable and the feature

00:05:48.060 --> 00:05:50.730
set that it provides to
enable Steve and his team

00:05:50.730 --> 00:05:53.560
to do the analytics at scale.

00:05:53.560 --> 00:05:54.930
So what is Bigtable?

00:05:54.930 --> 00:05:56.700
Bigtable is a
sparse, distributed,

00:05:56.700 --> 00:05:58.670
persistent,
multidimensional sorted map.

00:05:58.670 --> 00:06:01.980
That's the description that you
can see in the paper from 2006.

00:06:01.980 --> 00:06:04.590
So let's dive into details
for a bunch of these different

00:06:04.590 --> 00:06:08.710
attributes and see how
they work in practice.

00:06:08.710 --> 00:06:11.091
Bigtable is a distributed
key multivalue store.

00:06:11.091 --> 00:06:13.590
It is sparse in the sense that
unwritten cells don't take up

00:06:13.590 --> 00:06:15.289
any space, so you
don't have to fill out

00:06:15.289 --> 00:06:16.830
every single cell
in every single row

00:06:16.830 --> 00:06:18.830
for every single column family.

00:06:18.830 --> 00:06:21.312
It supports atomic
single row transactions

00:06:21.312 --> 00:06:23.520
so you can modify any values
across any of the column

00:06:23.520 --> 00:06:24.240
families.

00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:26.281
And it has a single index,
which is the row keys,

00:06:26.281 --> 00:06:28.650
so there are no secondary
indices for any of the columns

00:06:28.650 --> 00:06:30.550
that you see except
for the row key.

00:06:30.550 --> 00:06:32.400
And in this table,
you can see how

00:06:32.400 --> 00:06:35.700
you would access the
different cells in the table

00:06:35.700 --> 00:06:38.460
by using the row key, column
family, and column qualifier

00:06:38.460 --> 00:06:41.061
to uniquely identify them.

00:06:41.061 --> 00:06:43.830
Cloud Bigtable supports the
multi-dimensional map model

00:06:43.830 --> 00:06:44.850
as follows.

00:06:44.850 --> 00:06:47.670
For a tuple of
values, the row, which

00:06:47.670 --> 00:06:50.250
is a string, a column, which
is a combination of column

00:06:50.250 --> 00:06:52.380
family and an optional
column qualifier,

00:06:52.380 --> 00:06:55.726
together with an N64 timestamp,
given all of those values,

00:06:55.726 --> 00:06:56.850
you can retrieve the value.

00:06:56.850 --> 00:07:00.490
And the value is an
uninterpreted array of bytes.

00:07:00.490 --> 00:07:02.820
And so here you can see that
you have a built-in time

00:07:02.820 --> 00:07:06.300
dimension that you can
use to store time series.

00:07:06.300 --> 00:07:08.736
In fact, while most databases
are two-dimensional,

00:07:08.736 --> 00:07:10.360
Bigtable is actually
three-dimensional.

00:07:10.360 --> 00:07:12.570
So the third dimension
in Bigtable is time.

00:07:12.570 --> 00:07:14.810
Every cell has an arbitrary
number of versions,

00:07:14.810 --> 00:07:17.070
and there is an N64 attached
to each one of those cell

00:07:17.070 --> 00:07:17.790
versions.

00:07:17.790 --> 00:07:19.170
You can treat them as time.

00:07:19.170 --> 00:07:21.294
You can also treat them as
an arbitrary version ID,

00:07:21.294 --> 00:07:24.060
so you could just give them
numeric incrementing numbers.

00:07:24.060 --> 00:07:25.830
You can store an
arbitrary number of them,

00:07:25.830 --> 00:07:28.038
but you can also enable
automatic garbage collection,

00:07:28.038 --> 00:07:29.760
either based on time
or based on count.

00:07:29.760 --> 00:07:32.280
So you don't have to manually
go in and clean up and delete

00:07:32.280 --> 00:07:33.240
the unnecessary values.

00:07:33.240 --> 00:07:35.970
You can keep just writing
into Bigtable setup policy,

00:07:35.970 --> 00:07:38.850
and Bigtable will asynchronously
remove data after some time

00:07:38.850 --> 00:07:40.740
or after you've reached
a limit such as you

00:07:40.740 --> 00:07:43.554
say keep the last five values,
and Bigtable will automatically

00:07:43.554 --> 00:07:45.470
garbage collect them
asynchronously over time.

00:07:51.800 --> 00:07:54.450
Let's talk about the Request
Routing mechanism in Bigtable.

00:07:54.450 --> 00:07:56.620
Bigtable clients connect
to a single endpoint, which

00:07:56.620 --> 00:07:58.570
is the load balancing
proxy layer,

00:07:58.570 --> 00:08:00.760
and it then forwards the
request to the Bigtable

00:08:00.760 --> 00:08:02.164
nodes that serve the data.

00:08:02.164 --> 00:08:03.580
In the following
set of slides I'm

00:08:03.580 --> 00:08:05.200
going to remove
the load balancing

00:08:05.200 --> 00:08:06.239
proxy layer for clarity.

00:08:06.239 --> 00:08:08.530
But keep in mind that clients
don't necessarily connect

00:08:08.530 --> 00:08:09.821
directly to the Bigtable nodes.

00:08:09.821 --> 00:08:14.112
The requests are routed
through this API layer.

00:08:14.112 --> 00:08:16.570
So here you can see the clients
are connecting to Bigtable.

00:08:16.570 --> 00:08:18.130
Bigtable doesn't
actually store any data,

00:08:18.130 --> 00:08:19.963
which is kind of
interesting for a database.

00:08:19.963 --> 00:08:21.760
Bigtable itself is
stateless because it

00:08:21.760 --> 00:08:25.360
stores all the data in Colossus,
our distributed file system.

00:08:25.360 --> 00:08:27.940
Here you can see that a
Bigtable node manages data

00:08:27.940 --> 00:08:30.070
without holding onto
the data in Colossus.

00:08:30.070 --> 00:08:33.340
So Bigtable nodes have
exclusive read write access

00:08:33.340 --> 00:08:35.380
to sections of the
data in a table

00:08:35.380 --> 00:08:38.409
to provide the atomicity
of updates to a single row.

00:08:38.409 --> 00:08:40.450
So every row in
Bigtable is exclusively

00:08:40.450 --> 00:08:43.244
managed by at most one node.

00:08:43.244 --> 00:08:44.910
Nodes don't talk to
each other, so there

00:08:44.910 --> 00:08:47.090
are no cross-row
transactions in this case.

00:08:47.090 --> 00:08:49.650
But this allows for
variable scalability.

00:08:49.650 --> 00:08:53.160
Here the A, B, C, D, and
E are sorted key ranges

00:08:53.160 --> 00:08:54.110
within a single table.

00:08:54.110 --> 00:08:56.680
So as we mentioned
earlier, the storage model

00:08:56.680 --> 00:08:58.930
sorts the keys in the
table to allow you

00:08:58.930 --> 00:09:00.684
for fast linear scans.

00:09:00.684 --> 00:09:02.350
Now let's say we have
a single node that

00:09:02.350 --> 00:09:03.990
is getting a lot of
activity because there

00:09:03.990 --> 00:09:05.590
is a lot of popular
data that is being

00:09:05.590 --> 00:09:07.210
accessed by a bunch of clients.

00:09:07.210 --> 00:09:08.770
What Bigtable table
can do is simply

00:09:08.770 --> 00:09:11.320
reassign the ownership of
that data to a different node,

00:09:11.320 --> 00:09:14.326
and that is an online operation
without any data motion.

00:09:14.326 --> 00:09:16.450
So regardless of the
gigabytes or terabytes of data

00:09:16.450 --> 00:09:19.300
that you've just reassigned,
this is an online operation,

00:09:19.300 --> 00:09:21.280
and without any data
copying, the data

00:09:21.280 --> 00:09:23.314
stays exactly where
it was in Colossus,

00:09:23.314 --> 00:09:24.730
and this is just
a metadata update

00:09:24.730 --> 00:09:27.280
to show that there's
new owner of this data,

00:09:27.280 --> 00:09:28.974
and the requests
flow automatically.

00:09:28.974 --> 00:09:30.640
The client is completely
unaware of this

00:09:30.640 --> 00:09:32.140
because their requests
are automatically

00:09:32.140 --> 00:09:34.270
forwarded by the load balancing
proxy layer that I mentioned

00:09:34.270 --> 00:09:35.090
earlier.

00:09:35.090 --> 00:09:37.940
So clients don't need to do
any discovery or any updates

00:09:37.940 --> 00:09:39.940
or management of who do
they need to connect to.

00:09:39.940 --> 00:09:42.023
They have a single endpoint,
they connect to that,

00:09:42.023 --> 00:09:43.960
and the requests are
routed automatically,

00:09:43.960 --> 00:09:48.440
even when the data is rebalanced
and reassigned at scale.

00:09:48.440 --> 00:09:50.350
This ability to easily
reassign the data

00:09:50.350 --> 00:09:52.960
and the strong separation
between the processing storage

00:09:52.960 --> 00:09:56.264
enables the simple scalability
that Steve mentioned earlier.

00:09:56.264 --> 00:09:58.180
What this means is that
if we add another node

00:09:58.180 --> 00:09:59.970
to the cluster, as
you can see here,

00:09:59.970 --> 00:10:03.220
we can easily shard and reassign
the data to other nodes.

00:10:03.220 --> 00:10:07.069
And so the cluster will scale
in performance in linear order

00:10:07.069 --> 00:10:09.110
compared with the number
of nodes in the cluster.

00:10:09.110 --> 00:10:10.750
You don't have to do
anything to enable this.

00:10:10.750 --> 00:10:12.333
You just add more
nodes to the cluster

00:10:12.333 --> 00:10:14.620
and you get the additional
throughput in the cluster

00:10:14.620 --> 00:10:15.880
given the size of nodes.

00:10:15.880 --> 00:10:18.130
Similarly, of course, you
can shrink down the cluster,

00:10:18.130 --> 00:10:21.610
and we will again resize and
we balance the assignments

00:10:21.610 --> 00:10:23.650
to fit that smaller cluster.

00:10:23.650 --> 00:10:28.060
What this means is that you
can get seamless scalability

00:10:28.060 --> 00:10:31.900
in scaling for Cloud Bigtable
without any downtime.

00:10:31.900 --> 00:10:34.840
And as Steve alluded, you get
the linear scalability ability

00:10:34.840 --> 00:10:36.670
if you have good schema design.

00:10:36.670 --> 00:10:39.590
Now what does that mean to
have a good schema design?

00:10:39.590 --> 00:10:41.800
We'll talk about
this in a bit as

00:10:41.800 --> 00:10:43.474
to how you can get
good schema design

00:10:43.474 --> 00:10:45.140
and what it actually
means for Bigtable,

00:10:45.140 --> 00:10:46.567
and what performs well.

00:10:46.567 --> 00:10:49.150
But before we get to that, let's
talk about some of the recent

00:10:49.150 --> 00:10:51.644
features that we've
announced for Cloud Bigtable.

00:10:51.644 --> 00:10:53.560
Yesterday we announced
that Cloud Bigtable now

00:10:53.560 --> 00:10:57.340
supports replication, and it
is now generally available.

00:10:57.340 --> 00:10:59.500
There are no manual steps
to enable replication

00:10:59.500 --> 00:11:02.272
of synchronization, or
any steps to repair data,

00:11:02.272 --> 00:11:04.480
or anything to synchronize
on your writes or deletes.

00:11:04.480 --> 00:11:06.021
This is automatically
handled for you

00:11:06.021 --> 00:11:08.664
as soon as you add another
cluster to an instance.

00:11:08.664 --> 00:11:11.080
And some of the use cases that
you might consider for this

00:11:11.080 --> 00:11:13.510
are either high availability
for serving or workflow

00:11:13.510 --> 00:11:17.170
separation for batch
and serving use cases.

00:11:17.170 --> 00:11:19.390
As I mentioned, as
of yesterday, it

00:11:19.390 --> 00:11:22.740
increases the SLA
from 95 to 9995

00:11:22.740 --> 00:11:25.570
and a zero touch
failover for HA.

00:11:25.570 --> 00:11:28.450
So with that, let's return
to the row schema design

00:11:28.450 --> 00:11:30.764
and how that
impacts performance.

00:11:30.764 --> 00:11:32.680
As Steve mentioned, good
row key schema design

00:11:32.680 --> 00:11:35.680
is crucial for performance
to get the linear scalability

00:11:35.680 --> 00:11:37.145
because the row
key schema design

00:11:37.145 --> 00:11:38.770
will guide the access
patterns, and you

00:11:38.770 --> 00:11:41.186
want to make sure that your
access patterns scale linearly

00:11:41.186 --> 00:11:44.780
to enable linear scaling for
the performance in your Bigtable

00:11:44.780 --> 00:11:45.682
cluster.

00:11:45.682 --> 00:11:47.140
If you have uneven
access patterns,

00:11:47.140 --> 00:11:48.635
you'll end up with hotspots.

00:11:48.635 --> 00:11:51.010
If you're storing too much
data due to the row key schema

00:11:51.010 --> 00:11:54.250
design in a single row, you
will also you have trouble doing

00:11:54.250 --> 00:11:57.310
that because since we cannot
split a single row across

00:11:57.310 --> 00:12:00.430
multiple nodes in Bigtable, you
will have single servers that

00:12:00.430 --> 00:12:03.250
are really trying to serve
a single role that maybe

00:12:03.250 --> 00:12:04.349
gigabytes in size.

00:12:04.349 --> 00:12:06.640
So in the end, you really
want balanced access banners,

00:12:06.640 --> 00:12:08.692
you want reasonably
sized rows, and that

00:12:08.692 --> 00:12:10.150
will provide the
linear scalability

00:12:10.150 --> 00:12:12.730
that you have with Bigtable.

00:12:12.730 --> 00:12:14.870
Traditional monitoring
involves line charts,

00:12:14.870 --> 00:12:18.370
such as this one, where you
can look at CPU utilization,

00:12:18.370 --> 00:12:21.680
you can see latency, you can
see data stored and such.

00:12:21.680 --> 00:12:23.920
So this will show you,
for example, this graph,

00:12:23.920 --> 00:12:26.600
CPU utilization on
average in a cluster.

00:12:26.600 --> 00:12:28.810
We also have CPU utilization
for the hottest node

00:12:28.810 --> 00:12:32.170
in the cluster, which might hint
at the possibility of hotspots.

00:12:32.170 --> 00:12:33.754
But what if you
wanted to know more?

00:12:33.754 --> 00:12:35.920
What if you wanted to see
what is actually happening

00:12:35.920 --> 00:12:38.545
under the covers, and how is it
that you can improve your row

00:12:38.545 --> 00:12:41.710
key schema design to
improve performance?

00:12:41.710 --> 00:12:43.419
How would you know if
you had large rows?

00:12:43.419 --> 00:12:45.335
How would you know that
there's a map produced

00:12:45.335 --> 00:12:47.170
that may be affecting
your serving latency?

00:12:47.170 --> 00:12:49.630
How would you debug if
you really have high CPU

00:12:49.630 --> 00:12:52.225
and find what the cause
of that high CPU might be?

00:12:52.225 --> 00:12:53.600
With that, I want
to hand it over

00:12:53.600 --> 00:12:56.505
to Wim, who will talk about
a new feature for Cloud

00:12:56.505 --> 00:12:57.560
Bigtable, Key Visualizer.

00:12:57.560 --> 00:12:58.430
Wim?

00:12:58.430 --> 00:12:59.960
WIM DE PAUW: Thank you, Misha.

00:12:59.960 --> 00:13:01.530
Good morning, everyone.

00:13:01.530 --> 00:13:02.630
My name is Wim de Pauw.

00:13:02.630 --> 00:13:05.120
I'm the Engineering Manager
of the Visual Analytics

00:13:05.120 --> 00:13:09.950
team in New York and very proud
to announce Key Visualizer

00:13:09.950 --> 00:13:11.780
for Cloud Bigtable.

00:13:11.780 --> 00:13:13.520
This is a novel
approach to analyze

00:13:13.520 --> 00:13:16.190
the behavior of your table.

00:13:16.190 --> 00:13:18.020
You saw the line chart before.

00:13:18.020 --> 00:13:21.680
Well, typical line charts,
they only show one dimension.

00:13:21.680 --> 00:13:25.190
We show read and write access
patterns, both over time

00:13:25.190 --> 00:13:27.290
and the keyspace.

00:13:27.290 --> 00:13:30.080
This will help you find
and prevent hotspots.

00:13:30.080 --> 00:13:32.660
You can find rows
with too much data,

00:13:32.660 --> 00:13:35.000
and you can see if your
key schema is balanced.

00:13:37.760 --> 00:13:40.660
This is the heat map
if Key Visualizer.

00:13:40.660 --> 00:13:43.000
It will show time horizontally.

00:13:43.000 --> 00:13:45.070
It proceeds from left to right.

00:13:45.070 --> 00:13:49.720
This example shows one week
of behavior of your Bigtable.

00:13:49.720 --> 00:13:52.840
The keyspace is shown
vertically with the lowest

00:13:52.840 --> 00:13:57.240
keys at the bottom and the
highest keys at the top.

00:13:57.240 --> 00:13:59.370
Then there's the heat scale.

00:13:59.370 --> 00:14:03.390
You can see it has colors
from cold, in black, for zero,

00:14:03.390 --> 00:14:07.030
and then it goes to
dark blue, red, yellow,

00:14:07.030 --> 00:14:10.440
to glowing white hot for
extremely high values.

00:14:13.040 --> 00:14:16.160
In this example here, you
can see a diurnal pattern.

00:14:16.160 --> 00:14:18.670
You see this lowest
key range over here?

00:14:18.670 --> 00:14:21.170
Well, there's about
seven hotspots here.

00:14:21.170 --> 00:14:25.460
So someone must have hammered
that key range very heavily

00:14:25.460 --> 00:14:28.190
every night.

00:14:28.190 --> 00:14:31.280
Over there you see a cold
area because it's practically

00:14:31.280 --> 00:14:33.850
completely dark.

00:14:33.850 --> 00:14:38.830
And this horizontal line in the
middle that is glowing white

00:14:38.830 --> 00:14:43.350
must be a key that has been
used very, very heavily.

00:14:43.350 --> 00:14:46.330
In this example here,
you see a diagonal line,

00:14:46.330 --> 00:14:52.200
which is probably a sequential
scan from a MapReduce job.

00:14:52.200 --> 00:14:54.210
And then, if in Key
Visualizer, if you

00:14:54.210 --> 00:14:57.000
hover with the mouse
over the screen,

00:14:57.000 --> 00:15:01.980
you'll see in the tool tip more
details like the actual value,

00:15:01.980 --> 00:15:04.650
the precise time,
and then the start

00:15:04.650 --> 00:15:07.190
and the end key of the region
that you're hovering over.

00:15:10.350 --> 00:15:12.420
Around the heat map, you'll
see at the bottom end

00:15:12.420 --> 00:15:16.080
on the right aggregate
values in bar charts.

00:15:16.080 --> 00:15:17.970
So here at the bottom
you see averages

00:15:17.970 --> 00:15:22.260
over divisible keyspace
in 15-minute intervals.

00:15:22.260 --> 00:15:24.180
This is pretty much
what you would see

00:15:24.180 --> 00:15:27.990
in your traditional line chart.

00:15:27.990 --> 00:15:31.470
On the right side here,
there's another bar chart

00:15:31.470 --> 00:15:33.630
that shows you averages
over visible time

00:15:33.630 --> 00:15:36.090
range per key range.

00:15:36.090 --> 00:15:37.830
You see that spike over here?

00:15:37.830 --> 00:15:42.377
Remember, that was that bright
white line of that hot key

00:15:42.377 --> 00:15:43.460
that we had in the middle.

00:15:46.360 --> 00:15:49.990
Now when we were observing
Bigtable application developers

00:15:49.990 --> 00:15:54.340
at Google, we observed that
very often, although not always,

00:15:54.340 --> 00:15:57.250
they were creating their
keys in a hierarchical value,

00:15:57.250 --> 00:15:59.360
in a hierarchical way.

00:15:59.360 --> 00:16:03.100
So they were using prefixes and
concatenating these prefixes

00:16:03.100 --> 00:16:05.340
a little bit like this example.

00:16:05.340 --> 00:16:08.100
This example here uses
slashes and dashes.

00:16:08.100 --> 00:16:10.420
It could be other symbols, too.

00:16:10.420 --> 00:16:12.910
And so we thought, we're
going to, in Key Visualizer,

00:16:12.910 --> 00:16:17.920
extract the prefix tree so that
you can see your key schema

00:16:17.920 --> 00:16:20.810
in the heat map.

00:16:20.810 --> 00:16:25.250
So the result of that will
look something like that.

00:16:25.250 --> 00:16:27.890
So here you see an icicle chart.

00:16:27.890 --> 00:16:31.520
And the leftmost
tall, thin rectangle

00:16:31.520 --> 00:16:34.130
here shows you the top prefix.

00:16:34.130 --> 00:16:37.610
And then as you go right,
you see prefixes going down

00:16:37.610 --> 00:16:39.860
the hierarchy.

00:16:39.860 --> 00:16:40.910
Why is this useful?

00:16:40.910 --> 00:16:45.230
Well, first of all, you can now
see your key schema of the data

00:16:45.230 --> 00:16:46.940
that you designed.

00:16:46.940 --> 00:16:49.460
And hopefully you can
recognize the data

00:16:49.460 --> 00:16:51.630
that you stored that way.

00:16:51.630 --> 00:16:56.060
The other thing is that we
often observed that behavior is

00:16:56.060 --> 00:16:59.310
correlated with the key layout.

00:16:59.310 --> 00:17:03.200
So in this example here, this
is a very small key range,

00:17:03.200 --> 00:17:07.579
and you see that corresponds to
a series of key prefixes here.

00:17:07.579 --> 00:17:09.160
And it's a very
distinct pattern.

00:17:09.160 --> 00:17:14.210
You see this on and off pattern
over four days over here,

00:17:14.210 --> 00:17:16.085
all correlated with
the key prefix.

00:17:18.640 --> 00:17:22.270
Key Visualizer gives
you 11 metrics.

00:17:22.270 --> 00:17:25.750
First of all, we give you
ops, which is the sum of reads

00:17:25.750 --> 00:17:27.250
and writes.

00:17:27.250 --> 00:17:29.140
Then we give you
three warning metrics

00:17:29.140 --> 00:17:32.530
that can point you to
possibly bad performance.

00:17:32.530 --> 00:17:34.750
I'll talk about that later on.

00:17:34.750 --> 00:17:37.690
Then we have reads, writes.

00:17:37.690 --> 00:17:41.380
We have performance
metrics like latency.

00:17:41.380 --> 00:17:45.230
And finally, we have the amount
of data that is stored per row.

00:17:48.050 --> 00:17:51.110
Let me show you now some
common key access patterns.

00:17:51.110 --> 00:17:54.560
So over here you see
an imbalanced keyspace.

00:17:54.560 --> 00:17:56.990
Again, remember, time
goes horizontally

00:17:56.990 --> 00:17:58.750
from left to right.

00:17:58.750 --> 00:18:02.540
And you see that about
20% of the top keys

00:18:02.540 --> 00:18:04.120
here were very heavily used.

00:18:04.120 --> 00:18:10.140
They're glowing yellow and red,
whereas the 80% at the bottom,

00:18:10.140 --> 00:18:13.730
they're barely used because you
can see that it's very cold.

00:18:13.730 --> 00:18:16.790
In this example, you see that
your database was pretty much

00:18:16.790 --> 00:18:19.290
cold over the
whole key spectrum.

00:18:19.290 --> 00:18:21.770
But then all of a
sudden, at this time,

00:18:21.770 --> 00:18:25.340
you see a very sudden increase
for a key range from here

00:18:25.340 --> 00:18:27.750
to here.

00:18:27.750 --> 00:18:29.277
When we look at
that example here,

00:18:29.277 --> 00:18:30.860
you see that everything
is pretty much

00:18:30.860 --> 00:18:35.390
cold except for a few
glowing white keys.

00:18:35.390 --> 00:18:39.810
So that's an example of a couple
of hotspots that we see here.

00:18:39.810 --> 00:18:41.982
And then finally
in this example,

00:18:41.982 --> 00:18:43.940
this is the same example
that I pointed you out

00:18:43.940 --> 00:18:45.120
in the previous slides here.

00:18:45.120 --> 00:18:46.970
You see this
diagonal line, which

00:18:46.970 --> 00:18:51.770
is probably a sequential read
caused by a MapReduce job.

00:18:54.570 --> 00:18:58.320
Now Key Visualizer is not
just about visualization.

00:18:58.320 --> 00:19:02.730
It's also about analysis,
analysis on possibly trillions

00:19:02.730 --> 00:19:05.720
of metric values.

00:19:05.720 --> 00:19:10.750
So imagine if you have a
database with a trillion rows.

00:19:10.750 --> 00:19:14.710
If we want to show that
behavior on your laptop,

00:19:14.710 --> 00:19:16.660
we have to downsample
and aggregate it

00:19:16.660 --> 00:19:19.270
in one way or another
to get it to your laptop

00:19:19.270 --> 00:19:20.900
in less than a minute.

00:19:20.900 --> 00:19:22.750
So we have to aggregate
and downsample

00:19:22.750 --> 00:19:26.380
all these performance metrics.

00:19:26.380 --> 00:19:30.100
Then, on the other
hand, we also analyze

00:19:30.100 --> 00:19:34.420
every single performance value
that we collect in the system,

00:19:34.420 --> 00:19:36.640
and they're very detailed.

00:19:36.640 --> 00:19:39.800
The result of that
is three metrics.

00:19:39.800 --> 00:19:42.980
The first one is the
read pressure index.

00:19:42.980 --> 00:19:46.450
And that is a function, an
opaque function of read CPU,

00:19:46.450 --> 00:19:48.640
read queue length,
which is the number

00:19:48.640 --> 00:19:52.600
of read operations waiting
to be executed, and latency.

00:19:52.600 --> 00:19:56.020
And that function can point
you to possibly bad read

00:19:56.020 --> 00:19:58.030
performance.

00:19:58.030 --> 00:20:01.060
We have something similar
for write behavior.

00:20:01.060 --> 00:20:03.940
And then finally,
we have large rows

00:20:03.940 --> 00:20:06.250
which will point you to
any rows that contain

00:20:06.250 --> 00:20:10.080
more than one gigabyte of data.

00:20:10.080 --> 00:20:13.160
Now, this is a massive amount
of calculations and data

00:20:13.160 --> 00:20:14.630
that we analyze.

00:20:14.630 --> 00:20:16.670
But the good news
is that this has

00:20:16.670 --> 00:20:20.300
absolutely no performance
impact on your cluster,

00:20:20.300 --> 00:20:21.710
absolutely not.

00:20:21.710 --> 00:20:24.760
And it's all for free.

00:20:24.760 --> 00:20:26.850
This is an example here
of a high read pressure.

00:20:26.850 --> 00:20:31.040
You see this glowing spot.

00:20:31.040 --> 00:20:33.800
Now some of you may
think, well, this

00:20:33.800 --> 00:20:36.710
may be a little bit overwhelming
because I have 11 metrics.

00:20:36.710 --> 00:20:37.700
I have all that data.

00:20:37.700 --> 00:20:39.800
Where do I start?

00:20:39.800 --> 00:20:42.210
We made it easy for you.

00:20:42.210 --> 00:20:45.020
So when you open Key
Visualizer, it opens with ops.

00:20:45.020 --> 00:20:47.570
That's the default metric.

00:20:47.570 --> 00:20:50.900
And what it shows you is just
an overview of your activity.

00:20:50.900 --> 00:20:54.500
You see if there's any hot
regions, any cold regions,

00:20:54.500 --> 00:21:00.140
if you have any diurnal
patterns, any linear scans.

00:21:00.140 --> 00:21:03.980
And then you'll see if there's
any warnings at the top bar.

00:21:03.980 --> 00:21:07.760
If there aren't any
warnings, that's good news.

00:21:07.760 --> 00:21:09.290
Then there's nothing to do.

00:21:09.290 --> 00:21:11.340
You can just relax.

00:21:11.340 --> 00:21:14.030
If there are warnings,
and they will show up

00:21:14.030 --> 00:21:16.280
like this example
over here, then you

00:21:16.280 --> 00:21:18.680
click on the button
to see which keys

00:21:18.680 --> 00:21:22.499
and when they were
causing problems.

00:21:22.499 --> 00:21:24.040
I'm going to show
you an example now.

00:21:24.040 --> 00:21:27.630
So imagine you open Key
Visualizer in the ops view.

00:21:27.630 --> 00:21:29.960
You see an overview
of your behavior.

00:21:29.960 --> 00:21:31.360
This is one week.

00:21:31.360 --> 00:21:33.370
And you see what's
hot and what is cold,

00:21:33.370 --> 00:21:35.830
and the diurnal patterns.

00:21:35.830 --> 00:21:40.420
And over there, which I've
annotated in the red rectangle,

00:21:40.420 --> 00:21:42.970
you see this read
pressure warning.

00:21:42.970 --> 00:21:46.690
It has three exclamation marks,
so that means it's severe.

00:21:46.690 --> 00:21:50.680
I click on that button, and
it brings me immediately

00:21:50.680 --> 00:21:54.180
to the read pressure
index metric.

00:21:54.180 --> 00:21:58.610
You see this glowing
white stripe over here?

00:21:58.610 --> 00:22:02.540
So when I hover over
it with the tool tip,

00:22:02.540 --> 00:22:06.280
I see immediately the
details of the problems.

00:22:06.280 --> 00:22:07.800
So I've got a list
of problems here.

00:22:07.800 --> 00:22:10.130
But the most severe one
is the one at the top

00:22:10.130 --> 00:22:13.610
here, which I've annotated
with the red rectangle.

00:22:13.610 --> 00:22:16.184
And I'm going to
zoom into that now.

00:22:16.184 --> 00:22:16.850
So what is this?

00:22:16.850 --> 00:22:20.390
Well, you see it's
glowing, so it's bad.

00:22:20.390 --> 00:22:24.270
So first of all,
this is a single key.

00:22:24.270 --> 00:22:26.630
And it's been stressed
for two hours and a half.

00:22:26.630 --> 00:22:28.355
So that's pretty long.

00:22:28.355 --> 00:22:29.730
The other thing
which you can see

00:22:29.730 --> 00:22:32.970
is the exact name of the
offending key, shown over here

00:22:32.970 --> 00:22:35.040
in the second line.

00:22:35.040 --> 00:22:37.350
And why do we think
that it's bad?

00:22:37.350 --> 00:22:39.800
Well, look at the
total read CPU.

00:22:39.800 --> 00:22:45.420
It's using 446 millinodes,
which is roughly half a server

00:22:45.420 --> 00:22:46.890
worth of CPU.

00:22:46.890 --> 00:22:50.370
Remember, this is
just for one key.

00:22:50.370 --> 00:22:53.460
And then we also give
the potential culprit,

00:22:53.460 --> 00:22:57.270
the suggestion of what
probably was causing this.

00:22:57.270 --> 00:22:59.304
Well, we're dealing
with a large row here.

00:22:59.304 --> 00:22:59.970
So look at this.

00:22:59.970 --> 00:23:02.490
This is 10.38 gigabyte.

00:23:02.490 --> 00:23:04.870
And you know that
the recommended row

00:23:04.870 --> 00:23:09.750
size, the maximum row size in
Bigtable, is 256 megabytes.

00:23:09.750 --> 00:23:11.580
So this is way too
much, of course.

00:23:11.580 --> 00:23:14.790
But you imagine someone is
hammering that key very hard,

00:23:14.790 --> 00:23:16.800
pulling out all that data,
and each time they're

00:23:16.800 --> 00:23:19.440
pulling out 10 gigabytes.

00:23:19.440 --> 00:23:23.220
No wonder that this is
going to use a lot of CPU.

00:23:23.220 --> 00:23:25.890
See, this is how easy it
was, I open Key Visualizer.

00:23:25.890 --> 00:23:30.420
I look at the overview,
click on the alert buttons,

00:23:30.420 --> 00:23:34.470
and it shows me the problem.

00:23:34.470 --> 00:23:36.770
What we've shown you
so far are metrics

00:23:36.770 --> 00:23:43.190
that are views that are showing
one metric, for example, ops.

00:23:43.190 --> 00:23:45.170
And you can imagine
that you're looking

00:23:45.170 --> 00:23:47.720
at, say, a read hot spot.

00:23:47.720 --> 00:23:49.340
And you may want
to know, well, am I

00:23:49.340 --> 00:23:53.150
also writing at this
time and space and place?

00:23:53.150 --> 00:23:54.470
And you switch to writing.

00:23:54.470 --> 00:23:56.810
And then you want to see
is there any latency,

00:23:56.810 --> 00:23:58.670
and you switch to latency.

00:23:58.670 --> 00:24:00.380
And so this was a
little bit cumbersome.

00:24:00.380 --> 00:24:03.180
We thought to make this
a little bit easier.

00:24:03.180 --> 00:24:05.870
So this new feature,
which we probably

00:24:05.870 --> 00:24:08.630
will launch in a couple
of weeks from now,

00:24:08.630 --> 00:24:10.460
is the Multiple Metrics View.

00:24:10.460 --> 00:24:13.670
So here in the middle, you
see what we had before.

00:24:13.670 --> 00:24:17.460
When I hover with my
cursor over an area,

00:24:17.460 --> 00:24:19.670
I see in the tool
tip the values.

00:24:19.670 --> 00:24:23.540
But now, at the same time,
I see all the metric values

00:24:23.540 --> 00:24:28.380
for each metric over
there on the right side.

00:24:28.380 --> 00:24:31.070
So this lets you compare
and correlate metrics

00:24:31.070 --> 00:24:34.890
simultaneously just in one view.

00:24:34.890 --> 00:24:36.620
In this example here,
you see that the

00:24:36.620 --> 00:24:40.640
reads that are in purple,
they're somewhat lukewarm,

00:24:40.640 --> 00:24:43.400
whereas the writes,
they're bright yellow.

00:24:43.400 --> 00:24:45.500
So we're doing not so
much reading, but a lot

00:24:45.500 --> 00:24:48.600
of writing in this example.

00:24:48.600 --> 00:24:50.810
And then there's the Expand
All button over there.

00:24:50.810 --> 00:24:53.720
So if I click on that
one, this will bring up

00:24:53.720 --> 00:24:57.320
miniature views for
each metric that

00:24:57.320 --> 00:25:00.740
are showing the same
window as the main view.

00:25:00.740 --> 00:25:03.290
So here I had ops.

00:25:03.290 --> 00:25:06.470
Over there I had the
number of bytes written.

00:25:06.470 --> 00:25:10.670
Over here I have the
number of data stored,

00:25:10.670 --> 00:25:13.080
and here the number of rows.

00:25:13.080 --> 00:25:16.340
And if I hover with the
cursor over the main view,

00:25:16.340 --> 00:25:20.300
the cursor will simultaneously
change over here,

00:25:20.300 --> 00:25:22.820
and you see the
corresponding metrics also.

00:25:22.820 --> 00:25:25.460
If I zoom in and out, and
if I pan in the main view,

00:25:25.460 --> 00:25:29.300
all of these view will
just move and will

00:25:29.300 --> 00:25:32.750
be zooming in synchronized.

00:25:32.750 --> 00:25:36.700
So that will be coming
in a couple of weeks.

00:25:36.700 --> 00:25:40.960
Let me summarize to give you,
again, a couple of the benefits

00:25:40.960 --> 00:25:42.280
that I've demonstrated so far.

00:25:42.280 --> 00:25:46.930
So it helps you detect and
prevent key access hotspots.

00:25:46.930 --> 00:25:50.850
It helps you find large
rows more than one gigabyte.

00:25:50.850 --> 00:25:53.850
And there's no performance
impact for you at all.

00:25:53.850 --> 00:25:56.550
And it's free.

00:25:56.550 --> 00:25:59.370
One use case that I did
not illustrate over here

00:25:59.370 --> 00:26:02.820
is how to optimize
your key schema.

00:26:02.820 --> 00:26:04.470
And that's what
Steve is now going

00:26:04.470 --> 00:26:06.310
to talk about
because he used Key

00:26:06.310 --> 00:26:07.545
Visualizer for that purpose.

00:26:10.899 --> 00:26:11.940
STEVE NIEMITZ: Thank you.

00:26:14.970 --> 00:26:17.700
So as Wim introduced,
now we're going

00:26:17.700 --> 00:26:20.280
to talk about how we used
this feature while we were

00:26:20.280 --> 00:26:24.840
developing our analytics
product in order to tune

00:26:24.840 --> 00:26:29.500
and really optimize the data.

00:26:29.500 --> 00:26:32.760
So one of the things we realized
while we were building this

00:26:32.760 --> 00:26:35.070
is that the key schema
and the row key schema

00:26:35.070 --> 00:26:38.730
really matters a lot for
how well the performance

00:26:38.730 --> 00:26:41.790
of your Bigtable will be.

00:26:41.790 --> 00:26:44.850
Again, poor key schema
design will end up

00:26:44.850 --> 00:26:49.800
with a very nonscalable
Bigtable design.

00:26:49.800 --> 00:26:52.740
So a few common things that
we realized we needed to do

00:26:52.740 --> 00:26:57.750
is that we should tune our
data to the query patterns

00:26:57.750 --> 00:27:01.000
that we were expecting,
which kind of makes sense.

00:27:01.000 --> 00:27:04.080
So in building our
row keys, we basically

00:27:04.080 --> 00:27:06.990
want the queries, the
dimensions in those queries,

00:27:06.990 --> 00:27:09.997
to match the row key
from left to right.

00:27:09.997 --> 00:27:12.330
So you add some dimensions,
you put them in your row key

00:27:12.330 --> 00:27:13.926
in a serialized form.

00:27:13.926 --> 00:27:15.300
You want to be
able to match them

00:27:15.300 --> 00:27:23.380
left to right to get optimum
data querying in your queries.

00:27:23.380 --> 00:27:25.900
Also, you want your row
keys to be well distributed

00:27:25.900 --> 00:27:27.550
across the whole keyspace.

00:27:27.550 --> 00:27:30.760
Otherwise you're not going to
have a very linearly scalable

00:27:30.760 --> 00:27:33.040
system because only
certain nodes will be

00:27:33.040 --> 00:27:35.830
serving a majority of the data.

00:27:35.830 --> 00:27:39.490
And also, you want your queries
to have the minimum number

00:27:39.490 --> 00:27:43.870
of scans required in order
to keep your commonly

00:27:43.870 --> 00:27:46.230
accessed data together.

00:27:46.230 --> 00:27:47.730
So what we're going
to do now is I'm

00:27:47.730 --> 00:27:50.794
going to introduce just
an example, a kind of toy

00:27:50.794 --> 00:27:52.710
key schema that's a
simplified version of what

00:27:52.710 --> 00:27:55.470
we use for our actual
advertiser analytics.

00:27:55.470 --> 00:27:58.170
And we'll walk through some
simulated traffic on it

00:27:58.170 --> 00:28:01.380
and see what they looked
like in Key Visualizer,

00:28:01.380 --> 00:28:05.290
and how we improved the design.

00:28:05.290 --> 00:28:07.860
So here are some
simple dimensions.

00:28:07.860 --> 00:28:10.440
You can imagine
advertiser ID is just

00:28:10.440 --> 00:28:13.630
the ID we give any advertiser
that signs up with us.

00:28:13.630 --> 00:28:16.140
A campaign ID is a
campaign they're running.

00:28:16.140 --> 00:28:19.000
So maybe it's an ad they're
showing, something like that.

00:28:19.000 --> 00:28:24.270
Timestamp is the time the data
that we're querying corresponds

00:28:24.270 --> 00:28:24.960
to.

00:28:24.960 --> 00:28:27.820
Generally we group things
into an hour granularity.

00:28:27.820 --> 00:28:30.390
So the timestamp will
be at the hour level.

00:28:30.390 --> 00:28:33.300
Engagement type is just kind
of like what the user did

00:28:33.300 --> 00:28:34.350
to engage with the ad.

00:28:34.350 --> 00:28:36.630
Did they view it,
did they click on it,

00:28:36.630 --> 00:28:39.500
did they install an application,
something like that.

00:28:39.500 --> 00:28:42.360
And then display location
is where it was on the page.

00:28:42.360 --> 00:28:44.700
Was it in their timeline,
was it a search result,

00:28:44.700 --> 00:28:47.200
things like that.

00:28:47.200 --> 00:28:50.320
So basically our first
iteration was really simple.

00:28:50.320 --> 00:28:53.370
So let's take all of these
dimensions concatenated

00:28:53.370 --> 00:28:56.700
together from left to right,
and that becomes our row key.

00:28:56.700 --> 00:28:59.870
So timestamp, advertiser
ID, campaign ID, et cetera,

00:28:59.870 --> 00:29:01.480
et cetera.

00:29:01.480 --> 00:29:02.580
There are some pros here.

00:29:02.580 --> 00:29:05.550
It's really easy to scan
across all advertisers

00:29:05.550 --> 00:29:08.820
if you have a time range, and
generally all of our queries

00:29:08.820 --> 00:29:10.450
will have a time range in them.

00:29:10.450 --> 00:29:12.450
You have good data locality
because all the data

00:29:12.450 --> 00:29:14.670
for an advertiser
is grouped together.

00:29:14.670 --> 00:29:16.740
But now let's
check out the cons.

00:29:16.740 --> 00:29:21.920
So this is a view of
what our Key Visualizer

00:29:21.920 --> 00:29:23.610
looks like with this schema.

00:29:23.610 --> 00:29:24.600
This isn't great.

00:29:24.600 --> 00:29:29.700
So we have here, there's a big
spot of basically no traffic.

00:29:29.700 --> 00:29:33.020
Up here you can see there's
a very pronounced line

00:29:33.020 --> 00:29:36.330
of hot keys, which
generally will

00:29:36.330 --> 00:29:39.300
correspond to the current week
of data we're looking at here.

00:29:39.300 --> 00:29:41.280
Most of the queries for
our data that we have

00:29:41.280 --> 00:29:43.332
are querying for the
most recent data.

00:29:43.332 --> 00:29:44.040
That makes sense.

00:29:44.040 --> 00:29:45.831
People care more about
what's happening now

00:29:45.831 --> 00:29:47.557
than what happened in the past.

00:29:47.557 --> 00:29:48.390
So this isn't great.

00:29:48.390 --> 00:29:50.520
This is not a
really good design.

00:29:50.520 --> 00:29:52.410
If we had run this
in production,

00:29:52.410 --> 00:29:54.270
we'd probably have
very hot nodes

00:29:54.270 --> 00:29:56.100
all the time because
whatever node

00:29:56.100 --> 00:29:58.200
was hosting this small
key range in the middle

00:29:58.200 --> 00:30:01.690
here would be
overloaded all the time.

00:30:01.690 --> 00:30:02.570
So let's iterate.

00:30:02.570 --> 00:30:05.800
Let's take what we learned
here and see what we can do.

00:30:05.800 --> 00:30:09.690
So we learned timestamp was
not great to put in the front.

00:30:09.690 --> 00:30:11.970
So let's swap timestamp
and advertiser ID.

00:30:11.970 --> 00:30:13.920
What this is going to
do now is the advertiser

00:30:13.920 --> 00:30:16.770
ID is at the
beginning of my key.

00:30:16.770 --> 00:30:18.940
And the timestamp
is within there.

00:30:18.940 --> 00:30:21.900
So it's still easy to
scan for a time range

00:30:21.900 --> 00:30:23.610
within an advertiser.

00:30:23.610 --> 00:30:25.890
It's harder to query
for all advertisers.

00:30:25.890 --> 00:30:28.560
However, in our use cases,
that's not really something

00:30:28.560 --> 00:30:30.090
we care about.

00:30:30.090 --> 00:30:32.370
Advertisers coming in are
only looking at their data.

00:30:32.370 --> 00:30:35.460
Obviously we're not going to
let them query for anything.

00:30:35.460 --> 00:30:38.730
So although we have a
con here, it's not really

00:30:38.730 --> 00:30:39.700
the end of the world.

00:30:39.700 --> 00:30:41.820
It doesn't really fit
our use case anyways.

00:30:41.820 --> 00:30:44.850
So let's look at
what happened here.

00:30:44.850 --> 00:30:46.200
We got a little better.

00:30:46.200 --> 00:30:49.270
So there's more distribution.

00:30:49.270 --> 00:30:52.860
However, we still have some
hot keys, some hot ranges here.

00:30:52.860 --> 00:30:54.570
These most likely
will correspond

00:30:54.570 --> 00:30:59.040
to advertisers that are
more frequently querying

00:30:59.040 --> 00:31:00.040
for their data.

00:31:00.040 --> 00:31:03.210
Additionally, our
advertiser ID keys might not

00:31:03.210 --> 00:31:06.160
be very well distributed
across the entire keyspace.

00:31:06.160 --> 00:31:08.100
They're probably
sequentially generated

00:31:08.100 --> 00:31:10.200
integers or something.

00:31:10.200 --> 00:31:12.960
So in that case, we'll end
up with groupings of like,

00:31:12.960 --> 00:31:16.440
we probably generated a bunch
of IDs here and then here.

00:31:16.440 --> 00:31:19.330
And those are all
groups that add factors.

00:31:19.330 --> 00:31:21.630
So let's iterate again.

00:31:21.630 --> 00:31:24.690
We've learned that we put
advertiser ID in front,

00:31:24.690 --> 00:31:26.860
things worked out
a little better.

00:31:26.860 --> 00:31:28.620
However, we didn't get
a good distribution

00:31:28.620 --> 00:31:31.260
on that advertiser ID keyspace.

00:31:31.260 --> 00:31:33.600
So instead, let's reverse
the advertiser ID.

00:31:33.600 --> 00:31:36.720
Basically the first digit
is now the last digit, which

00:31:36.720 --> 00:31:39.054
will basically
synthetically distribute

00:31:39.054 --> 00:31:40.470
the keys a lot
better because now,

00:31:40.470 --> 00:31:43.577
instead of the most significant
bit that's generally not

00:31:43.577 --> 00:31:45.660
changing much, you have
the least significant bit,

00:31:45.660 --> 00:31:48.100
which is always
cycling all the time

00:31:48.100 --> 00:31:51.120
if they're sequentially
generated IDs.

00:31:51.120 --> 00:31:53.230
So pros and cons are
basically the same now.

00:31:53.230 --> 00:31:55.530
There's not a big
difference other

00:31:55.530 --> 00:31:58.200
than you couldn't scan
advertiser IDs sequentially.

00:31:58.200 --> 00:31:59.300
But we don't care.

00:31:59.300 --> 00:32:01.270
That's not a use case anyways.

00:32:01.270 --> 00:32:03.400
So this is what we end up with.

00:32:03.400 --> 00:32:05.450
Everything looks pretty good.

00:32:05.450 --> 00:32:09.160
We still have some rows here
which correspond, probably,

00:32:09.160 --> 00:32:12.520
to more frequently
accessed advertisers that

00:32:12.520 --> 00:32:13.690
are pulling their data more.

00:32:13.690 --> 00:32:15.460
But in general, the
keyspace is really

00:32:15.460 --> 00:32:21.200
well distributed across
basically the entire range.

00:32:21.200 --> 00:32:23.690
This is what we want
it to look like.

00:32:23.690 --> 00:32:27.460
And this will lead to a
really well-performing design.

00:32:27.460 --> 00:32:28.900
So the really
important thing here

00:32:28.900 --> 00:32:31.090
is because we were
able to do this,

00:32:31.090 --> 00:32:34.450
it really gave us confidence
that when we took this product

00:32:34.450 --> 00:32:36.730
and went live with it,
that we wouldn't end up

00:32:36.730 --> 00:32:38.666
with horrible
performance issues,

00:32:38.666 --> 00:32:40.540
and we wouldn't have to
redesign it in a year

00:32:40.540 --> 00:32:43.150
because it wouldn't scale.

00:32:43.150 --> 00:32:45.160
It's also been
really helpful for us

00:32:45.160 --> 00:32:46.870
if we have a production
issue come up,

00:32:46.870 --> 00:32:50.890
like why is our latency going
up for certain advertisers,

00:32:50.890 --> 00:32:51.760
or things like that.

00:32:51.760 --> 00:32:53.489
We can go right
into Key Visualizer

00:32:53.489 --> 00:32:55.030
and see what's
happening at the time.

00:32:57.730 --> 00:33:00.830
And now I'm going to
hand it over to Misha.

00:33:00.830 --> 00:33:03.320
MISHA BRUKMAN: So as you've
seen, Steve at Twitter

00:33:03.320 --> 00:33:05.350
was able to use
Bigtable at the scale

00:33:05.350 --> 00:33:07.450
that Twitter needs to
do visual analytics

00:33:07.450 --> 00:33:09.460
and serve their ads analytics.

00:33:09.460 --> 00:33:12.010
Bigtable provides the fully
managed, NoSQL database

00:33:12.010 --> 00:33:13.480
to serve these
types of use cases

00:33:13.480 --> 00:33:16.530
for time series user
analytics as well as others.

00:33:16.530 --> 00:33:18.190
It provides
petabyte-scale scalability

00:33:18.190 --> 00:33:21.790
for storage, high throughput and
low latency for random access.

00:33:21.790 --> 00:33:26.560
And it provides the ability
to seamlessly redistribute

00:33:26.560 --> 00:33:29.110
the data to handle
increases in load,

00:33:29.110 --> 00:33:32.080
and seamless scalability
while adjusting

00:33:32.080 --> 00:33:34.240
to the access patterns
that you can put on it.

00:33:34.240 --> 00:33:37.980
And you can learn more
about it at the URL.

00:33:37.980 --> 00:33:40.100
WIM DE PAUW: So Key
Visualizer, which

00:33:40.100 --> 00:33:42.200
is in beta now for
Cloud Bigtable,

00:33:42.200 --> 00:33:44.790
visualizes your key
access patterns.

00:33:44.790 --> 00:33:47.720
It will help you find
hotspots and large rows.

00:33:47.720 --> 00:33:49.070
And it's not just visualization.

00:33:49.070 --> 00:33:52.190
We also analyze trillions
of metric values.

00:33:52.190 --> 00:33:56.430
It extracts the key prefix
hierarchy if it's available.

00:33:56.430 --> 00:33:59.030
And it doesn't have any
impact on the performance

00:33:59.030 --> 00:34:00.530
of your cluster.

00:34:00.530 --> 00:34:06.169
There's a URL here that you
can go to for more information.

00:34:06.169 --> 00:34:08.460
STEVE NIEMITZ: And then
finally, some of the really key

00:34:08.460 --> 00:34:11.130
features that Bigtable
and Key Visualizer

00:34:11.130 --> 00:34:13.409
enabled for us at Twitter.

00:34:13.409 --> 00:34:14.909
It really gave us
a very fast time

00:34:14.909 --> 00:34:17.280
to market on bringing in
this new improved advertiser

00:34:17.280 --> 00:34:20.580
analytics product up to scale.

00:34:20.580 --> 00:34:23.070
Additionally, using
Key Visualizer

00:34:23.070 --> 00:34:25.889
gave us really good
confidence that we

00:34:25.889 --> 00:34:29.790
would be able to scale up
and end up with a scalably

00:34:29.790 --> 00:34:31.250
designed system.

00:34:31.250 --> 00:34:34.199
We were able to iterate
on the row key design way

00:34:34.199 --> 00:34:35.699
faster than we would
have been if we

00:34:35.699 --> 00:34:40.560
had to infer it through other
metrics like P99 latency,

00:34:40.560 --> 00:34:42.040
things like that.

00:34:42.040 --> 00:34:43.770
And then finally, it
made it really easy

00:34:43.770 --> 00:34:46.395
to change the row key
schema during development

00:34:46.395 --> 00:34:47.520
and just see what happened.

00:34:47.520 --> 00:34:49.800
It let us iterate
really quickly,

00:34:49.800 --> 00:34:51.389
but without going
live in production

00:34:51.389 --> 00:34:55.139
and actually having to
worry about impacting that.

00:34:55.139 --> 00:34:56.190
So that's all we have.

00:34:56.190 --> 00:34:57.720
Thank you very much.

00:34:57.720 --> 00:35:00.770
